  "notes": "After the defense takes the first 3 spade tricks, and returns a club, South needs the rest of the tricks. Only six tricks are available in top winners (4 hearts, the ♦A, and the ♣A). One of the advantages of a trump contract, however, is that extra tricks are available by ruffing.\n\nBy employing the cross-ruff technique, South can score 8 trump tricks to go with the ♦A and ♣A. In order to do this, South must not draw any trumps.\n\nAfter the ♣A and ♦A are cashed, the next 8 tricks will consist of club ruffs by North and diamond ruffs by South. The only danger remaining is that East will overruff North's ♥3 or that West will overruff South's ♥2. To minimize this risk, South should take these two ruffs first. After that the defense is helpless as all of the remaining North and South hearts are high.\n\nThe defense would have done better to lead trumps after cashing their 3 spade tricks. Due to the poor splits in clubs and diamonds, the contract would fail.",
